# Sang Logium – Cline / agent rules

You are working on a production Next.js 15 (App Router) + React 19 + TypeScript e-commerce site for high-end audio gear (Sanity CMS, Stripe Payment Intents, Better Auth, Zustand, Tailwind, Playwright + Vitest).

**This file and `AGENTS.md` are the complete rule set for Cline.** Do not read or apply
`CLAUDE.md`, `.devin/`, or `.windsurf/` unless a task explicitly points you to a specific
file in them. They target other agents and will only add conflicting instructions.

## FEEDBACK LOOP — HARD GATES (never break; breaking = defect)

G0 PLAN-THEN-STOP. Before ANY tool call: ≤3-line plan + one go/no-go question.
   No exploration, no reads, no bd commands until the human replies.

G1 CHECKPOINT EVERY UNIT. Emit one user-facing line (done / next / blocker)
   after EVERY logical unit of work. NEVER accumulate more than 3 tool calls
   in a row without a user-facing line. A response with only tool calls and
   no checkpoint line = violation.

G2 NO SELF-TOKEN-METER. The agent has NO view of its token/quota usage.
   "Minimal tokens" is enforced by checkpoint FREQUENCY, never by the agent
   claiming it watched its own usage. When in doubt: stop and ask.

G3 SAMPLE BEFORE SCALING. Before a batch of N (files/issues/edits), do 1,
   show the concrete result, get go/no-go. Never fan out first.

Per-turn budget contract: state the tool-call count planned for this turn up
front; the human gates by count, not by token % (the agent has no usage view).

## Issue tracker = beads (`bd` CLI)

Work is tracked in **beads**, driven from the terminal with `bd`. Issue IDs look like
`sang-logium-69m`. The `.beads/` folder is a Dolt DB + generated export — to READ, CLAIM,
UPDATE or CLOSE an issue, NEVER grep/read/edit files inside `.beads/`; always go through `bd`.
(Fast issue *creation* via a direct `issues.jsonl` append is the one sanctioned exception —
see `.devin/rules/fast-beads-issue-creation.md`.)

**Titles are mandatory-structured** — see `_project/beads-naming-convention.md`. Epic =
`EPIC Filters Sorting`; child = `[Filters] Price min/max <-> URL`; standalone =
`Search: clamp out-of-range ?page=`. Never a raw ID in a title. Reference an issue as
`` `sang-logium-agq` — EPIC Filters Sorting `` (ID + title), never the bare ID.

- `bd show <id>` — read an issue. Its GOAL + "Current status:" IS the full spec. The goal
  is always end-user UX acceptance tests — `When I <interaction>, then <observable outcome>`
  lines for a human check on `localhost:3000` — never prose or `file:line`. Mandatory, not
  opt-in. (Contradicts `.devin/workflows/beads-issue-gate.md`'s heavy anatomy — this wins.)
- `bd ready` — list open, unblocked issues
- `bd update <id> --claim` — take an issue (sets assignee + `in_progress`)
- `bd note <id> "…"` — append a progress/evidence note
- `bd close <id>` — finish it (no "done" status exists; lifecycle is `open → in_progress → closed`)
- After any `bd` write: `bd export -o .beads/issues.jsonl` and commit that file with your code

To implement an issue end-to-end, invoke the `/implement-beads-issue <id>` skill.

Hard limits (obey strictly):
- ABSOLUTE BAN, NO EXCEPTIONS — self-verification commands. NEVER run `tsc`, `next build`, `next lint`, `eslint`, `npm run build`, `npm run lint`, `npm run test`, `vitest`, `playwright test`, `npm run dev`/`next dev` (starting a new dev server), `curl` against the dev server, Lighthouse, or any other build/type-check/lint/test command to check your own work. Not "just to be safe," not because a workflow file or an issue's acceptance criteria seems to call for it. The only verification that counts: (1) the human runs the live check on `localhost:3000`, or (2) a human/agent reads the diff. The ONLY way this lifts: the human, in the live conversation, explicitly asks you to run one of these commands right now. Violating it is a serious defect, not a judgment call.
- NEVER use $(...) or backticks in terminal commands. Write a temporary .js/.ps1/.sh script and run that instead.
- ONE COMMAND AT A TIME. No parallel fallbacks. Verify tools exist first with a single which/--version check.
- NEVER run other expensive commands (npm install, whole-project lint beyond the ban above) unless the user explicitly asks or it is genuinely unavoidable. Prefer targeted reads, grep, git status, isolated checks. Ask first.
- Any className edit touching height/sizing (h-full, min-h-, max-h-, aspect-) under app/components/** must be reviewed against docs/vertical-space-lg-touch.md before the task is done.

Architecture quick map:
- app/(store) public storefront, (admin), (studio) Sanity, checkout/, actions/, api/, components/, lib/, hooks/
- Catalogue is Build-Time VFS (data/catalogue.ts + prebuild script) – do not turn it into live per-request Sanity queries.
- Sanity queries live only under sanity-cms/
- Prefer existing patterns: Zod + React Hook Form, Zustand, server actions, iron-session for checkout cookies, Upstash Redis for inventory, etc.

Coding standards:
- TypeScript strict, no any.
- Tailwind only for styling; follow existing component patterns.
- Mobile-first + the lg-touch breakpoint rules.
- Keep diffs small and focused. Prefer edit over rewrite.
- After any non-trivial change, tell the human the one minimal check to run on localhost:3000. Do NOT run tsc, tests, or lint yourself.

Cost & context discipline:
- Never dump whole node_modules, large generated files, or unneeded directories.
- Ignore the root `*-master-execution-prompt.txt` / `.md` files unless a task names one — they are human copy-paste sources, not agent rules.
- Use @file or directory mentions for relevant context only.
- Keep system / rules text static at the top so DeepSeek prompt caching hits hard.
- For routine UI, catalogue, simple CRUD, tests → prefer Flash model.
- For complex multi-file refactors, race conditions, checkout/auth edge cases → switch to Pro or ask user.

When stuck or hallucinating: stop, summarize state, ask for clarification or start a New Task.

## Clickable file links

When outputting a file or directory path, always print it as a `file://` URI (e.g. `file:///home/jan/file.json`) for one-click terminal opening.